(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-prof-shah-style-quiz"]={"title":"Prof. Shah Style Integration Set 1 — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Which muscle is innervated by the peroneal (fibular) nerve, originates from the proximal tibia, inserts on the medial cuneiform and the base of the 1st metatarsal, and dorsiflexes the foot?","choices":["Tibialis posterior","Gastrocnemius","Soleus","Tibialis anterior"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tibialis anterior is the anterior-compartment dorsiflexor: origin on the proximal tibia, insertion on the medial cuneiform and 1st metatarsal base, and peroneal (fibular) nerve innervation. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which muscle lies in the deep posterior compartment of the leg, plantar flexes AND inverts the foot, and shares the medial malleolar tunnel with the flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus?","choices":["Tibialis posterior","Fibularis longus","Fibularis brevis","Tibialis anterior"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tibialis posterior plantar flexes and inverts the foot and passes behind the medial malleolus with the flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus (the 'Tom, Dick, and Harry' of the deep posterior compartment). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which muscle is innervated by the long thoracic nerve, protracts the shoulder and rotates the scapula so the glenoid cavity moves superiorly, and produces a 'winged scapula' when paralyzed?","choices":["Rhomboid major","Serratus anterior","Trapezius","Pectoralis minor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Serratus anterior is supplied by the long thoracic nerve, protracts the shoulder and upwardly rotates the scapula, and a long thoracic nerve lesion causes the winged scapula. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which gluteal-group muscle extends the hip and has an origin spanning the iliac crest to the sacrum with insertion on the iliotibial tract and the gluteal tuberosity of the femur?","choices":["Gluteus medius","Gluteus minimus","Tensor fasciae latae","Gluteus maximus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gluteus maximus extends the hip; its origin spans the iliac crest to the sacrum and it inserts on the iliotibial tract and the gluteal tuberosity of the femur. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which set of three muscles converges to insert at the medial tibia to form the pes anserinus?","choices":["Biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus","Vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and rectus femoris","Sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus","Gluteus maximus, medius, and minimus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pes anserinus is formed where the gracilis, sartorius, and semitendinosus converge and insert at the medial tibia. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle laterally (externally) rotates the arm at the shoulder and lies in the infraspinous fossa on the posterior scapula?","choices":["Infraspinatus","Supraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres major"],"answer":0,"exp":"Infraspinatus occupies the infraspinous fossa and laterally rotates the arm at the shoulder as part of the rotator cuff. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle has a long head that originates on the scapula (infraglenoid tubercle), inserts on the olecranon of the ulna, and extends the forearm at the elbow?","choices":["Biceps brachii","Brachialis","Triceps brachii","Brachioradialis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The triceps brachii (long head from the infraglenoid tubercle of the scapula) inserts on the olecranon and extends the forearm at the elbow. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which forearm muscle flexes the wrist AND adducts it toward the ulnar side, sitting in the anterior superficial compartment of the forearm?","choices":["Flexor carpi radialis","Extensor carpi ulnaris","Extensor carpi radialis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Flexor carpi ulnaris flexes and adducts (ulnar deviates) the wrist and belongs to the anterior superficial compartment of the forearm. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscle closes the eye and is innervated by cranial nerve VII (the facial nerve)?","choices":["Orbicularis oculi","Levator palpebrae superioris","Superior rectus","Corrugator supercilii"],"answer":0,"exp":"Orbicularis oculi closes the eye and, like all muscles of facial expression, is innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle rotates the eye downward and laterally and is innervated by cranial nerve IV (the trochlear nerve)?","choices":["Inferior oblique","Lateral rectus","Inferior rectus","Superior oblique"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior oblique rotates the eye downward and laterally and is the sole muscle supplied by the trochlear nerve (CN IV) — the 'SO4' of LR6(SO4)3. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication opens the jaw and is innervated by the mandibular branch of cranial nerve V (the trigeminal nerve)?","choices":["Masseter","Temporalis","Lateral pterygoid","Medial pterygoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Among the muscles of mastication, the lateral pterygoid opens the jaw; all are innervated by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle is the exception innervated by cranial nerve X (the vagus) rather than cranial nerve XII, and elevates the tongue?","choices":["Genioglossus","Hyoglossus","Styloglossus","Palatoglossus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Palatoglossus is the lone tongue muscle supplied by CN X (vagus) and elevates the tongue; the others take CN XII. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which neck muscle is innervated by cranial nerve XI (the accessory nerve) and, acting on one side, turns the head toward the opposite side?","choices":["Omohyoid","Sternocleidomastoid","Sternohyoid","Thyrohyoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the accessory nerve (CN XI); acting unilaterally it turns the face toward the opposite side and both together flex the neck. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which muscle is innervated by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), originates from the xiphoid process, ribs 7–12, and the lumbar spine, and inserts on a central tendon?","choices":["Rectus abdominis","External oblique","Diaphragm","Internal intercostal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The diaphragm arises from the xiphoid process, ribs 7–12 and their costal cartilages, and the lumbar spine, inserts on its central tendon, and is innervated by the phrenic nerves (C3–C5). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which muscle depresses the ribs and flexes the vertebral column, originating from the superior surface of the pubis and inserting on the inferior surfaces of ribs 5–7 and the xiphoid process?","choices":["Rectus abdominis","Transversus abdominis","External oblique","Diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Rectus abdominis originates from the superior pubis, inserts on the inferior surfaces of ribs 5–7 and the xiphoid process, depresses the ribs, and flexes the vertebral column; it contains the linea alba and tendinous inscriptions. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which muscle elevates the ribs, running from the inferior border of each rib to the superior border of the rib below?","choices":["Internal intercostal","External intercostal","Transversus thoracis","Serratus posterior inferior"],"answer":1,"exp":"External intercostals run from the inferior border of one rib to the superior border of the rib below and elevate the ribs (inspiration). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which erector spinae muscle is the MOST MEDIAL of the three columns and extends the vertebral column?","choices":["Longissimus","Iliocostalis","Multifidus","Spinalis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Within the erector spinae group, spinalis is the most medial column, longissimus is intermediate, and iliocostalis is most lateral; all extend the vertebral column. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which bone is a large sesamoid bone with an apex and base, whose posterior surface bears concave facets that articulate with the femoral condyles?","choices":["Patella","Talus","Calcaneus","Navicular"],"answer":0,"exp":"The patella is a large sesamoid bone with an apex and base; its concave posterior facets articulate with the medial and lateral condyles of the femur, and it protects the knee. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which forearm bone is medial to its partner, bears the trochlear notch that pivots on the trochlea of the humerus, and has the olecranon and coronoid process proximally?","choices":["Ulna","Radius","Humerus","Scaphoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ulna is medial to the radius; its trochlear notch pivots on the trochlea of the humerus and its proximal end carries the olecranon and coronoid process. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"On which bone does the radial groove (for the radial nerve) run, between the greater tubercle proximally and the capitulum and trochlea distally?","choices":["Ulna","Humerus","Radius","Clavicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The humerus bears the radial groove (radial nerve) on its shaft, the greater/lesser tubercles and intertubercular sulcus proximally, and the capitulum and trochlea distally. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which scapular feature is the shallow socket on the lateral angle that forms the glenohumeral joint with the head of the humerus?","choices":["Glenoid cavity","Coracoid process","Acromion","Subscapular fossa"],"answer":0,"exp":"The glenoid cavity sits at the lateral angle of the scapula and articulates with the head of the humerus to form the glenohumeral joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which femoral feature is the roughened ridge on the POSTERIOR shaft that serves as a major muscle-attachment line?","choices":["Intertrochanteric line","Linea aspera","Intercondylar fossa","Fovea"],"answer":1,"exp":"The linea aspera is the roughened ridge on the posterior shaft of the femur and is a major muscle-attachment site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which coxal-bone feature is the large opening bounded by the pubis and ischium, and which structure articulates with the head of the femur?","choices":["Greater sciatic notch is the opening; the ischial tuberosity articulates with the femoral head","Obturator foramen is the opening; the acetabulum articulates with the femoral head","Iliac fossa is the opening; the ischial spine articulates with the femoral head","Acetabular notch is the opening; the auricular surface articulates with the femoral head"],"answer":1,"exp":"The obturator foramen is the large opening framed by the pubis and ischium, while the acetabulum is the cup that receives the head of the femur. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which set lists the FOUR proximal carpal bones (lateral to medial)?","choices":["Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate","Scaphoid, lunate, capitate, hamate","Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","Trapezium, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform"],"answer":2,"exp":"The proximal row (lateral to medial) is scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform — the first half of 'Sally left the party to take Cathy home.' — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which cranial bone contains the sella turcica, the greater and lesser wings, and the foramen ovale and foramen spinosum?","choices":["Ethmoid","Occipital bone","Sphenoid","Temporal bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sphenoid houses the sella turcica, has greater and lesser wings, and is pierced by the foramen ovale, foramen spinosum, and foramen rotundum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which opening transmits the medulla oblongata, the accessory nerve (CN XI), and the vertebral arteries?","choices":["Optic canal","Foramen magnum","Foramen ovale","Jugular foramen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The foramen magnum of the occipital bone transmits the medulla oblongata, the accessory nerve (CN XI), and the vertebral arteries. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which vertebra has NO body, has the largest vertebral foramen, articulates with the occipital condyles, and permits the 'yes' nodding motion?","choices":["Axis (C2)","Atlas (C1)","Vertebra prominens (C7)","Thoracic vertebra (T6)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The atlas (C1) lacks a body, has the largest vertebral foramen, articulates with the occipital condyles, and allows the head to nod 'yes.' — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which bone forms the FLOOR of the bony orbit?","choices":["Frontal bone","Zygomatic bone","Sphenoid","Maxilla"],"answer":3,"exp":"The maxilla forms the floor of the orbit; the frontal bone forms the roof, the sphenoid the posterior wall, the zygomatic the lateral wall, and the lacrimal/ethmoid the medial wall. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which cranial suture lies between the occipital bone and the two parietal bones?","choices":["Sagittal suture","Coronal suture","Lambdoid suture","Squamous suture"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lambdoid suture is between the occipital bone and the two parietal bones. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which ribs are the 'true' (vertebrosternal) ribs that connect directly to the sternum via their own costal cartilages?","choices":["Ribs 1–7","Ribs 8–10","Ribs 8–12","Ribs 11–12"],"answer":0,"exp":"True (vertebrosternal) ribs are 1–7, attaching to the sternum by their own costal cartilages; false ribs are 8–12 and floating ribs are 11–12. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"}]};
